azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

Been a while since I've looked at these....

> [1]: An emergency way to blind yourself might be to stab your eyes out,
> like Oedipus. Great for things with blinding attacks, and you don't
> have to be a motherf.. to do it! --TheShrewdDude

I'd say no, it's too limited-application.  However, I do have to wonder
why one can't just CLOSE his eyes when around a monster with a gaze
attack....

> [2]: Tinned fruit. That's all we really need now, isn't it? Tinned
> fruit, mate. --TheShrewdDude

Not bad.  Not too compelling an addition, but whimsy is good.

> [3]: A friendly intelligent creature adjacent to you might perform the
> Heimlich maneuver if you're about to choke. Very handy. --TheShrewdDude

It shouldn't be something the player can rely upon (low chance of it
happening), but cool otherwise.  The player should get no benefit from
the food consumed afterwards, however, which *may* require some very
annoying rejiggering of the code.

> [4]: Snakes should hiss when you #chat with them. --BenUrban

Snakes don't always hiss, but then, dogs don't always bark and cars
don't always mew.  This logically should be there.

> [5]: Optional handicaps: the ability to start the game hungry or with
> eroded armor, for instance. --azgor

Those aren't much of a handicap, so it doesn't work for its stated
purpose.  And it'd make Nethack's extremely clean startup interface
cluttered for a fairly minor addition.

Unlike Angband, which puts new characters in a self-contained die
roller module and lets him keep going until he's satisfied with his
initial numbers, and ADOM, which can be even worse with its litany of
questions to ask about the life of a character that might die within
mere minutes, Nethack's current character creation is exactly right:
Name, Race, Class, Sex and Alignment, and we're off.

> [6]: Humanoid and demonic characters have the ability to sexually abuse
> you. You might meet a rapist on your way to minetown and break your
> chastity conduct, or perhaps Orcus might be interested in poking his
> wand where it doesn't belong. At any rate, you should bring protection
> or at least a blessed unicorn horn. --TheShrewdDude

Um, no.

> [7]: When hallucinating and hungry/weak from hunger, enemies should
> start appearing as %s, moreso when weak. (Just like in the cartoons!)
> If you're polymorphed and have a bite attack YAFMs ought to pop up too.
> --Patashu

I like this one (I also like Toon Hits), although "YAFMs ought to pop
up" is too vague and so this idea is incomplete.  What would the
messages be?

> [8]: Typo: "You should have stopped studying at vetenary." (should be
> veterinary) --BenUrban

Ah, You should send this to the Devteam, then.

> [9]: The lurker above digests you! You finish engraving. <-- There
> should be some way to remedy this situation (being engulfed while
> engraving). --BenUrban

Agreed.  Again, send to the Devteam!

> [10]: That lurker above didn't seem to be able to see my previously
> engraved Elbereths on that spot until after it regurgitated me; I would
> think it should be able to see them even while i'm adding more.
> --BenUrban

Of course it doesn't make sense for an engulfed creature to be able to
engrave, that's the real bug here.

> [11]: Stacking to avoid a surcharge should exercise wisdom. --BenUrban
> [12]: Stacking to add a surcharge should abuse wisdom. --BenUrban

I'm not familiar with this charge?  Anyway, the exercise system,
regarding Wisdom, works best when the game can unequivocally tell what
the player is trying to do, and whether that thing is positive or
negative.

> [13]: rocks fired from slings have a bonus against giants (David vs
> Goliath anyone?) --teabing

Not a bad idea.  (Is there one already?  I forget.)

> [14]: Mammalian pets successfully bitten by a rabid rat should have a
> small chance of becoming untame, unless you are a werecreature of the
> same class as the pet. --jackalbait

I like the idea of making a fairly generic monster more special, but it
should be a very small chance, lest the player's starting ally become a
general liability.

> [15]: You should always have the option of same-sex-sex. Demons of the
> same gender won't force it on you, but if you chat and ask nicely, you
> can even express your alterative sexual preferences in NetHack.
> --TheShrewdDude

This idea has come up before.  The problem here is one of balance:
what's to keep the player from "switch hitting" in order to get
benefits from whatever *cubus should be around?  These encounters can
grant experience levels, making them potentially unbalancing.  As it
is, one must be able to change sex to get benefit out of both types.

This is a case where Nethack's current design get in the way of adding
such a feature, so the succubus/incubus encounter results matrix would
probably have to be redesigned before this could be added, and that's
trickier.

> [16]: A cursed container (besides a bag of holding) should have a
> chance of cursing its contents every time it is used. --TheShrewdDude

I'd say no.  Cursed items generally do not have magical effects if the
original item is not magical.  It would also be quite open to abuse.

